Subject: winedmo: lock ASF startup timestamp base on the first timestamped packet
License: LGPL-2.1-or-later
Modification date: 2026-11-17

normalize_stream_timestamps() only set stream->timestamp_base_set when
base > 2000000 (200 ms), leaving normal ASF/WMV streams starting at
pts = 0 unlatched until a frame around 233 ms crossed the threshold.
Subtracting that mid-stream base jumped pts back to 0, causing
video_decoder.c to flush FFmpeg reference frames before a P-frame and
corrupt the picture as gray macroblock residuals.

Lock stream->timestamp_base_set on the first packet that carries a
timestamp (keeping timestamp_base = 0 when base <= 2000000) and skip
rebasing when timestamp_base is 0.

diff --git a/dlls/winedmo/unix_demuxer.c b/dlls/winedmo/unix_demuxer.c
index aa8fe07..a7a3ed8 100644
--- a/dlls/winedmo/unix_demuxer.c
+++ b/dlls/winedmo/unix_demuxer.c
@@ -188,14 +188,14 @@ static void normalize_stream_timestamps( struct stream *stream, struct sample *s
          * timestamp even though the clip itself starts at zero. Preserve normal
          * small startup offsets, but rebase obviously late first samples so MF does
          * not hold video for the first second or more of the clip. */
-        if (base != INT64_MIN && base > 2000000)
+        if (base != INT64_MIN)
         {
-            stream->timestamp_base = base;
+            stream->timestamp_base = base > 2000000 ? base : 0;
             stream->timestamp_base_set = TRUE;
         }
     }
 
-    if (!stream->timestamp_base_set)
+    if (!stream->timestamp_base_set || !stream->timestamp_base)
         return;
 
     if (sample->pts != AV_NOPTS_VALUE)
